* Numbers that can be negative.
*
* A cell counts from zero upwards and has no room for a sign, so a signed
* value here is *two* cells: `sign`, which is 1 when the value is negative and
* 0 when it is not, and `size`, which is how far from zero it is. A program
* says `@signed_add(x_sign, x_size, c_sign, c_size)` and never has to remember
* which is which.
*
* Sign and size rather than the wrapping arithmetic a machine uses, for one
* reason: multiplication. Multiplying two wrapped numbers means pulling the
* sign back out of each of them first, which costs a comparison against 128
* every time; multiplying these is multiplying the sizes and asking whether
* the signs agree. Addition pays for that -- it has a case for signs that
* agree and a case for signs that do not -- and addition is the cheaper thing
* to make awkward.
*
* Zero is written with a positive sign, and every answer is put back that way
* before it is handed over. Two places would otherwise produce a negative
* zero: `@signed_negate`, which leaves zero alone rather than flipping it, and
* `@signed_add`, whenever two sizes cancel and the first was negative. The
* second is the one a sweep found -- `-1 + 1` came out as a zero that compared
* unequal to zero.
*
* **This file owns cells 20 to 33**, the way `lib/fast.bfm` owns 0 to 19. The
* alternative is a macro with fourteen parameters, most of them scratch, which
* is not a library anybody would use. Two `at`s naming one cell are allowed on
* purpose, so nothing will tell you if a program pins one of these; pin nothing
* below 34 in a program that includes this file.

@include "idioms.bfm"

@var s_agree at 20
@var s_smaller at 21
@var s_keep at 22
@var s_keep2 at 23
@var s_count at 24
@var s_step at 25
@var s_passed at 26
@var s_flag at 27
@var s_other at 28
@var s_scratch at 29
@var s_spare at 30
@var s_sign at 31
@var s_size at 32
@var s_tmp at 33

* A signed value from a sign and a size written down.
*
*     sign, size = s, m
@macro signed_put(sign, size, s, m) {
    @put(sign, s)
    @put(size, m)
}

* The same value the other way up. Zero has no other way up, so it is left as
* it is -- otherwise nothing would compare equal to it.
*
*     if size:
*         sign = 1 - sign
@macro signed_negate(sign, size) {
    @is_not_zero(size, s_flag, s_scratch, s_spare)
    @to s_flag
    [
        @is_zero(sign, s_other, s_scratch, s_spare)
        @clear(sign)
        @to s_other
        [
            @put(sign, 1)
            @clear(s_other)
        ]
        @clear(s_flag)
    ]
}

* `a` gains `b`; `b` keeps its value.
*
*     if a_sign == b_sign:          # the same way: the sizes add
*         a_size += b_size
*     elif a_size < b_size:         # facing off, and b wins
*         a_sign, a_size = b_sign, b_size - a_size
*     else:                         # facing off, and a wins
*         a_size -= b_size
@macro signed_add(a_sign, a_size, b_sign, b_size) {
    @clear(s_agree)
    @copy(a_sign, s_agree, s_spare)
    @equal(s_agree, b_sign, s_scratch, s_spare)

    @when(s_agree, s_flag, s_scratch, s_spare) {
        @copy(b_size, a_size, s_spare)
    }

    @unless(s_agree, s_flag, s_scratch, s_spare) {
        @less(a_size, b_size, s_smaller, s_count, s_step, s_passed, s_flag, s_other, s_scratch, s_spare)

        @when(s_smaller, s_flag, s_scratch, s_spare) {
            * b is the larger, so the answer faces b's way and is b - a.
            @clear(s_keep)
            @copy(b_size, s_keep, s_spare)
            @to a_size
            [
                -
                @to s_keep
                -
                @to a_size
            ]
            @clear(a_size)
            @move(s_keep, a_size)
            @clear(a_sign)
            @copy(b_sign, a_sign, s_spare)
        }

        @unless(s_smaller, s_flag, s_scratch, s_spare) {
            * a is at least as large, so the answer keeps a's sign.
            @clear(s_keep)
            @copy(b_size, s_keep, s_spare)
            @to s_keep
            [
                -
                @to a_size
                -
                @to s_keep
            ]
        }
    }
    @clear(s_agree)

    * Nothing is negative. Two sizes that cancel would otherwise leave the
    * sign of whichever was written first.
    @when_zero_clear_sign(a_sign, a_size)
}

* Zero, written the one way it is written here.
@macro when_zero_clear_sign(sign, size) {
    @is_zero(size, s_tmp, s_scratch, s_spare)
    @to s_tmp
    [
        @clear(sign)
        @clear(s_tmp)
    ]
}

* `a` loses `b`; `b` keeps its value. Adding it the other way up, on a copy,
* so that `b` is left as it was found.
@macro signed_sub(a_sign, a_size, b_sign, b_size) {
    @clear(s_sign)
    @copy(b_sign, s_sign, s_spare)
    @clear(s_size)
    @copy(b_size, s_size, s_spare)
    @signed_negate(s_sign, s_size)
    @signed_add(a_sign, a_size, s_sign, s_size)
}
